# =============================================================================
# production.def — the conversion-graph node table (CraftingSystem, W0-5)
# =============================================================================
# The container the economy programme's conversion graph (ECONOMY.md §4) needs, and
# the one [recipes] cannot be: [recipes] is one row per workstation DeviceKind,
# single-input/single-output, so a second bill on the same station OVERWRITES the
# first. A [production] row is keyed by its own node ID instead, so many nodes may
# name one station, and it takes MANY inputs and MANY outputs.
#
# SHIPPED STATE (E0-6 + E0-7): this table is NO LONGER EMPTY. It was empty from W0-5
# until E0-6, and the THREE rows at the bottom of this file are now the authority for
# the SalvageRecycler, the Fabricator and the IceMelter. The MachineShop still has NO
# row and therefore still takes ProductionDefs.TryGetBill's fallback leg into [recipes]
# (Parts:2 -> ControllerModule:1, already a 50 % hop — E0-6 did not retune it).
#
# The melter is here and not in [recipes] because [recipes] cannot say what it needs to
# say: a melter's output is LITRES, not a stack, and only a [production] row may declare
# `none` outputs. The water arrives through WaterSystem off water.def's
# `ice_liters_per_unit` — see CraftingSystem's completion hook.
#
# The rows are VERBATIM equal to SimDefs.CreateDefault()'s d.Production, as the def
# contract requires (DefsEquivalenceTests compares the two sources bit for bit); the
# reasoning for each ratio is written out there and is not repeated here.
#
# SINCE E0-7 THIS SECTION IS AN OVERLAY, NOT A REPLACEMENT: DefsParser seeds the table
# from the compiled defaults, so a row whose id already exists RETUNES it in place, a new
# id APPENDS, and a data dir that omits this file keeps the compiled table. Content can no
# longer REMOVE a compiled node — the same contract [machines] and [recipes] already have.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COLUMNS (whitespace-aligned; five of them)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ID  STATION  WORK_S  INPUTS  OUTPUTS
#
#     ID        node key, unique in the table. A later row with the SAME id replaces
#               it in place (the .def overlay contract) — that is how a pack retunes
#               a core node without disturbing table order.
#     STATION   DeviceKind NAME of the workstation. NOT the key: several nodes may
#               name one station (but see NOT YET CONNECTED below).
#     WORK_S    whole seconds of work per batch, 1..86400 (tick count = work_s x
#               Simulation.TicksPerSecond).
#     INPUTS    'Kind:count' pairs joined by '+', NO spaces (the row is whitespace-
#               split). At least one port; a kind may not repeat within the list.
#               Consumed all-or-nothing when the batch starts; a worker fetches for
#               the first short port in port order.
#     OUTPUTS   same syntax, or the literal 'none' for a pure sink (ECONOMY.md §5 S7,
#               "vent the carbonate — gone forever"). All ports spawn at the worker's
#               tile when the batch completes.
#
# EVERY COLUMN IS AN INTEGER OR AN ENUM NAME. There is no yield/efficiency column,
# on purpose:
#
#   CONVERSION LOSS IS THE INPUT:OUTPUT RATIO. `Scrap:20 Regolith:17` is exactly
#   85 %, on every machine, forever. A fractional yield multiplier cannot be exact
#   against integer stacks — floor(n*y)/n equals y only when n*y is a whole number,
#   so 0.85 would need out_counts in multiples of 20 and ECONOMY.md §10's 0.93
#   reclaimer multiples of 100, and one node-level yield produces a DIFFERENT
#   effective rate per output port. Ratios are exact, culture-free and float-free,
#   the closed-mass axiom (§2.1) is checkable by reading the row, and the author has
#   to state the batch size that the rounding was going to impose on him anyway.
#
#   BUT THE BATCH SIZE IS ALSO A STAGING REQUIREMENT, and that is the real cost of a
#   fine-grained ratio. Inputs are all-or-nothing: CraftingSystem will not start a
#   batch until EVERY input port's full count is staged on the bench's neighbours,
#   and a worker fetches ONE stack per trip. So `Scrap:20 Regolith:17` makes the crew
#   stage twenty units before a single batch begins, where `Scrap:4` needed four —
#   five times the hauling round-trips per batch, and a correspondingly longer stretch
#   of bench idle time while they run. That lands directly on the labour model that
#   ECONOMY.md §12.1 A1 measures.
#
#   Two consequences for whoever tunes this table. First, scale `work_s` with the
#   batch: a 5x batch at unchanged `work_s` is a ~5x throughput change, not a
#   like-for-like re-expression of the same node. Second, prefer the COARSEST ratio
#   that says what you mean — 0.85 as `Scrap:20 Regolith:17` costs five times the
#   logistics of an 0.75 written `Scrap:4 Regolith:3`, so buy precision deliberately.
#
# ORDER IS SIGNIFICANT here, unlike [machines]/[recipes]: this section is an ordered
# list (rows accumulate across files in Ordinal filename order), the checksum folds
# it in that order, and a station's bill is resolved by ORDINAL within it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WORKED EXAMPLES — commented out on purpose (see SHIPPED STATE above)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ECONOMY.md §4/§10 shapes in this syntax, so the next lane has a concrete target.
# Uncommenting any of them CHANGES the game and the defs checksum — and the ones
# marked (E1+/E2+) also need ItemKind rows that do not exist yet. §3.2 staged Seals,
# Ice, Swarf and Circuits, each landing with its consumer: Seals landed with E0-6, Ice with
# E0-7 and SWARF with the wreck start — so only Circuits is still absent. Swarf's consumer is
# MaintenanceSystem, not a bench; see the block below.
#
# ⚠️ `recycle_swarf` BELOW IS NOT MERELY UNBUILT — IT IS UNBUILDABLE HERE, and the wreck
# start's salvage half is the package that found out. ItemKind.Swarf now EXISTS and a
# stripped wreck produces it, so the only thing between this row and a live swarf economy
# looks like uncommenting it. It is not:
#
#   ProductionDefs.TryGetBill resolves a station's bill at ORDINAL 0 and nothing else, and
#   ALL THREE benches already carry one — recycle_stock (SalvageRecycler), fab_components
#   (Fabricator), and the MachineShop's [recipes] row. A second node on any of them parses,
#   checksums, is reachable through TryGetNode and IS NEVER RUN. That is the `fab_seals`
#   trap this file already warns about, and it bites identically here.
#
#   Displacing an existing bill is not an option either: dropping recycle_stock kills
#   Regolith -> Scrap and with it every Part on every ship, and taking the MachineShop kills
#   ControllerModule. There are three stations and three conversions; a fourth conversion
#   needs a fourth station or a bill-SELECTION rule, and selection needs per-station save
#   state (which bill is mid-batch) — E-PROD's PROD blob, not a def edit.
#
#   DRIVEN, not read off the source: DefsProductionTests / SwarfSalvageTests plant a second
#   SalvageRecycler node with its inputs on the bench and measure that the batch never runs,
#   with the same node at ordinal 0 as the inclusion control.
#
# So the wreck's salvage feeds REPAIR instead of PRODUCTION: Swarf is consumed directly by
# MaintenanceSystem as the rung below a jury-rig (wear.swarf_service_condition). That is the
# stricter conservation story of the two — there is no Swarf -> Parts path at all — and the
# row below stays commented until one of the two exits above is built.
#
# [production]
# # id             station          work_s  inputs              outputs
# recycle_swarf    SalvageRecycler  20      Swarf:5             Regolith:3           # exactly 60%  (E1+)
# fab_seals        Fabricator       15      Regolith:1          Seals:2              # (a SECOND bill — never runs, see below)
# shop_controller  MachineShop      40      Parts:2+Circuits:1  ControllerModule:1   # (E2+)
# scrap_corpse     SalvageRecycler  60      Corpse:1            none                 # a pure SINK
#
# Note 'shop_controller': two INPUT ports. Note 'scrap_corpse': 'none' outputs.
# Note the ratios: matter leaves the ship at every hop, in exact whole units.
#
# ⚠️ 'fab_seals' above is the trap this section used to advertise as a feature. Two
# bills on ONE station parse and checksum, but only ORDINAL 0 is ever RUN (see NOT
# YET CONNECTED below), so declaring it would make Seals unreachable while looking
# correct. That is exactly why E0-6 ships Seals as a CO-OUTPUT of the Fabricator's
# one bill instead of as a second bill of its own.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT YET CONNECTED (MECHANICS.md §13.12)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CraftingSystem resolves a station's bill at ORDINAL 0 — the first node in table
# order naming that station. Rows beyond ordinal 0 parse, checksum and are reachable
# through ProductionDefs.TryGetNode, but nothing selects among them yet: choosing
# needs per-station state (which bill is mid-batch, priorities, quotas) and therefore
# a save chapter, which is the E-PROD lane's PROD blob. Declaring a second node on a
# station is NOT silent — the parser emits a problem line pointing here.

[production]
# id             station          work_s  inputs      outputs
recycle_stock    SalvageRecycler  2400    Regolith:4  Scrap:3
fab_components   Fabricator       900     Scrap:2     Parts:1+Seals:1
melt_ice         IceMelter        300     Ice:1       none
